{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe sibling admission runner fills fixture coverage once, at a single point in time. Two\nthings reopen gaps: a criterion added to the registry later starts with no fixture, and a\ncriterion whose candidates all failed to reproduce is left with none. Without a mechanism that\nrevisits those gaps, coverage decays from day one and the only repair is someone noticing. A\nrepair loop that spends money unattended is itself a hazard, so it needs a bound independent of\nanyone watching it.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Gap-only scope.** Each run considers only criteria that\n`criteria.effective_routing('.', gate_key='plan_review')` reports and for which no spec file\nexists under `.rebar/evals/` or `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/`. Criteria that already have a\nfixture are never revisited, so the loop's work shrinks monotonically and reaches zero when\nevery criterion is covered — unattended spend bounded by construction, not by a watchdog.\n\n**Quarantine breaker (a persistent ticket-based quarantine, not an in-memory breaker).**\nCloser to the reconciler's convergence breaker (`_config_schema`, ADR 0027/0028) than to a\nclassic in-memory breaker; the sibling a5bf records the pattern. Two triggers file a\nquarantine ticket titled exactly `unreliable-criterion: <criterion-id>` — the literal prefix\n`unreliable-criterion: ` (WITH the trailing space) then the registry criterion id:\n\n1. **Three consecutive failed runs.** The loop persists, per criterion, a consecutive-failure\n   counter in the loop's rebar state file (beside the due-stamp). A run is a failure for a\n   criterion when its emitted spec's candidates fail to reproduce, OR when the criterion is\n   reported `skipped-unbalanced` (no case on both sides of the fire/no-fire axis). On the\n   third such consecutive run the loop files the ticket. The counter RESETS to zero when the\n   criterion becomes covered (a fixture admits) or its quarantine ticket is closed.\n2. **Not inline-runnable (container/ISF-class).** Per the parent epic, a criterion whose\n   finder cannot run on inline plan text — the container criteria (G3/G4/decomp-shape) and the\n   ISF class — is quarantined immediately on first encounter with the same ticket, reason\n   \"finder not inline-runnable\"; the loop never spends on a criterion it cannot mine.\n\n**How the quarantine feeds the selector — the loop OWNS this wiring; it is NEW work, not\nexisting reuse.** `fixture_selection.select_candidates` does NOT read ticket titles: it skips\nonly criteria present in a caller-supplied `unreliable: dict[criterion_id -> ticket_id]` map\n(its internal reason string `unreliable-criterion:{ticket_id}` is keyed on ticket id, never\nparsed back, and is a DIFFERENT string from the title). This loop builds that map itself: it\nqueries OPEN tickets whose title matches `unreliable-criterion: <id>`, parses the criterion id\nfrom the title, and passes `{id: ticket_id}` to the selector. Ticket creation is IDEMPOTENT —\nthe loop files only when no OPEN ticket with that exact title exists — so overlapping runs\ncannot double-create, and the once-per-interval due-stamp serializes ordinary runs.\n\n**Portability.** Recurring work must not depend on a CI provider. The trigger is\noperation-linked with an in-process due-stamp: the loop records its last run in the\nrepository's rebar state, and a rebar plan-review operation advances it once the configured\ninterval (default 30 days) has elapsed. It is also directly invocable as a command, so an\noperator, a cron, or any CI provider can drive it. No step depends on a POSIX-only shell or a\nprocess-cwd `.rebar/` path.\n\n**Unattended spend bound.** Mining is free; only admission costs. Each run pre-flights through\n`ledger.reserve(..., cap_usd=25.0)` against the loop's own ledger and stops when remaining\nheadroom cannot cover the next criterion, leaving the rest for the next interval — bounding\nunattended spend to $25 per interval regardless of how many gaps exist.\n\n**Failure disposition.** A transient model/transport error in an admission attempt is\nRETRYABLE and retried within the run. `BudgetExceeded` is FATAL and non-retryable: the run\nstops with completed criteria admitted and their spend reconciled, and the next interval\nresumes. A criterion whose candidates fail to reproduce is not an error — it increments the\nbreaker counter and the run continues. When the two coincide, the ceiling wins.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe loop command, its due-stamp trigger and interval configuration, the per-criterion\nconsecutive-failure counter (in the loop's rebar state file), the open-ticket\ntitle→`unreliable` map builder that feeds the selector, both quarantine triggers (three\nconsecutive failures AND not-inline-runnable), the idempotent `unreliable-criterion:` ticket\ncreation, and the loop's own ledger file. It reuses the sibling selector, emitter and\nadmission runner.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Repairing a criterion recorded as unreliable; that is the work the filed ticket represents.\n- Revisiting criteria that already have a fixture, or removing/rewriting an existing fixture.\n- Any human curation step between mining and admission.\n\n## Compatibility and rollback\n\nAdditive: a new command, a new state stamp/counter, and a new ledger file. No existing\ncommand, spec, or persisted format changes. Back-out is deleting the command and its state;\nnothing reads that state if the command is absent, so removal leaves no data loss.\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit tests drive the breaker counter, gap-only selection, the title→`unreliable`-map builder,\nboth quarantine triggers, idempotent creation, the due-stamp arithmetic and the ledger stop\ncondition against a stubbed admission runner over a temporary store, deterministic without a\nmodel. A subprocess test covers the command with no CI environment.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A criterion that already has a spec file is absent from a run's attempt list; `pytest`\n      asserts the selected id set excludes it.\n- [ ] The third consecutive failed run creates exactly one ticket titled\n      `unreliable-criterion: <criterion-id>`, and the first and second do not; `pytest` against\n      a temporary store asserts the title and the ticket count after each run.\n- [ ] A run reported `skipped-unbalanced` increments the same counter as a reproduction\n      failure; `pytest` asserts the ticket is created after three runs of mixed failure kinds.\n- [ ] A criterion with an open ticket of that title is absent from the attempt list; `pytest`\n      asserts the id is not selected (the loop's title→`unreliable`-map builder queries the\n      open ticket and passes it to `select_candidates`).\n- [ ] A container/ISF-class criterion (finder not inline-runnable) is quarantined on FIRST\n      encounter — one ticket filed without three runs; `pytest` asserts the ticket exists after\n      a single run over a seeded such criterion.\n- [ ] Ticket creation is idempotent — a second run while the ticket is open creates no\n      duplicate; `pytest` asserts the ticket count stays 1 after a re-run.\n- [ ] A run stops before a criterion whose estimate exceeds remaining headroom and admits the\n      criteria it completed; `pytest` against the real `ledger.reserve` with `cap_usd=25.0`\n      asserts the admitted set and that the ledger total is at most 25.0.\n- [ ] The command runs with no CI environment variables set and reports its attempt list; a\n      `pytest` subprocess test with a cleared environment asserts this.\n- [ ] The due-stamp advances only after the configured interval; `pytest` asserts no attempt\n      at interval minus one day and one at the interval.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n`make lint`, `make typecheck` and `make test` pass; a run over a seeded gap attempts exactly\nthat gap; a seeded three-failure history produces the ticket and the skip.\n\n## Budget\n\n**Hard cap $100** for building this child and its first runs. Each unattended run is\nseparately capped at $25 by its own pre-flight, and the loop's total cost falls to zero once\nevery criterion is covered or recorded as unreliable."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788496309517415001,"uuid":"9105d9c0-293e-4068-94bc-b60056eedfef"}